1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,480
 That doesn't reflect reality,

2
00:00:01,480 --> 00:00:04,240
 this idea of very linear, predictable careers

3
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:06,620
 where there are enough promotions for everybody,

4
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:09,640
 that that doesn't actually reflect people's experience.

5
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:13,180
 And it also doesn't reflect people's individuality.

6
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:15,760
 People want to do different things.

7
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:19,240
 They don't necessarily want a career based on conformity.

8
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:21,800
 They want the choice to develop

9
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:23,200
 in the direction that's right for them.

10
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:24,240
 - Hello, everybody.

11
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:26,560
 Welcome to season four of Better at Work.

12
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:27,960
 We're delighted to be back with you.

13
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:29,320
 I'm Carl Quinlan.

14
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:30,960
 I've got some exciting news for you.

15
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:35,600
 We're now not just in your ears, but on your screens too.

16
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:38,400
 That's right, Better at Work is now available

17
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:40,720
 to watch on YouTube or Spotify.

18
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:44,660
 Explore our conversations with some remarkable guests.

19
00:00:44,660 --> 00:00:49,000
 And new this season, listeners questions episodes

20
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:51,520
 with my lovely co-host Annette

21
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:55,640
 on the Better at Work YouTube channel and Spotify.

22
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:57,400
 You can also simply keep listening

23
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:00,080
 wherever you get your podcasts.

24
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:02,120
 Now to kick off season four,

25
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:04,920
 I sat down with a very special guest,

26
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:08,240
 someone I've admired for a long time, Helen Topper.

27
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:11,520
 Now Helen is co-author of the Sunday Times bestseller,

28
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:13,480
 The Squiggly Career, and of course,

29
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:16,160
 she's CEO of Amazing If.

30
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:20,440
 Helen has helped thousands of people re-imagine their career

31
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:22,940
 from climbing that traditional ladder

32
00:01:22,940 --> 00:01:24,920
 to something far more exciting,

33
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:28,000
 full of possibility and squiggly.

34
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
 But when you flip to squiggly thinking,

35
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:32,880
 you don't talk about levels, you talk about learning,

36
00:01:32,880 --> 00:01:34,720
 you don't talk about grades, you talk about growth,

37
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:36,560
 you don't talk about titles, you talk about talents,

38
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:38,800
 you don't talk about plans, you talk about possibilities.

39
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:43,240
 It's just, it's a much more curious and open way

40
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:44,640
 of looking at your career.

41
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:46,240
 What I loved about this chat

42
00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:49,160
 is how practical Helen's advice is

43
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:50,840
 and how she takes us

44
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:53,960
 into her personal career transformation journey.

45
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:56,560
 She realized so many people are stuck

46
00:01:56,560 --> 00:01:58,840
 chasing ladders and titles,

47
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:02,560
 but what really holds us back isn't the structure,

48
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:04,720
 it's a lack of confidence.

49
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:08,480
 That was the game-changing realization in her own career.

50
00:02:08,480 --> 00:02:09,880
 If you've ever felt stuck

51
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:12,640
 or like someone else is actually writing your story,

52
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:14,480
 this episode is for you.

53
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:16,120
 This is a big one to have Helen on the show.

54
00:02:16,120 --> 00:02:18,600
 She's joining us here from our studios in London.

55
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:20,080
 Welcome, Helen, to Better at Work.

56
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:21,320
 Aw, thank you so much for having me.

57
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:22,360
 I can't wait for the conversation.

58
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:24,240
 It's such a long time we've been planning this.

59
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:26,600
 I know, I know, I'm really looking forward to it.

60
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:28,560
 Now look, we start all of our discussions

61
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:30,720
 with all of our guests the same way,

62
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:33,720
 which, where did it all start for you?

63
00:02:33,720 --> 00:02:36,240
 What happened in your early life

64
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:38,400
 or along the way that drove this interest

65
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:40,680
 in helping people have better careers?

66
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:42,560
 Yeah, I mean, it definitely didn't start with that.

67
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:45,920
 Selfishly, I think it was very, like, my career.

68
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:47,920
 I think that's definitely where it started.

69
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:51,760
 And I have always been very, very driven,

70
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:53,240
 very, very ambitious.

71
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:56,000
 And I think I saw the ladder

72
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,840
 as, like, the ideal version of success.

73
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:02,000
 And early on in my career,

74
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,280
 I think that was, like, indicated by, like, graduate schemes.

75
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:07,080
 I mean, graduate schemes are the way to go.

76
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:09,320
 In careers, particularly like traditional grad schemes,

77
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:10,360
 when careers look like ladders,

78
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:12,040
 you start on a grad scheme.

79
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:14,320
 And I like them so much, I did three back-to-back.

80
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:15,640
 You were joking.

81
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:16,480
 I loved it.

82
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:18,280
 I loved it 'cause it was like ready-made progression.

83
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:19,120
 Oh my God.

84
00:03:19,120 --> 00:03:21,600
 So I did this one that was, like, affiliated with my course,

85
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:23,920
 which is where I ended up meeting my business partner back.

86
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:27,480
 Like, this is 2001, where we kind of met each other.

87
00:03:27,480 --> 00:03:28,320
 2001, yeah.

88
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:29,440
 Gosh, gosh.

89
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:31,400
 Yeah, early, early days, we met each other there.

90
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:33,080
 I did, like, a boot sort of graduate scheme,

91
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:34,240
 an early version of it.

92
00:03:34,240 --> 00:03:35,560
 Then I went and did one at Britvic.

93
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:37,440
 Then I went and did an advanced graduate scheme

94
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:38,280
 at Procter & Gamble.

95
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:39,120
 And then I kind of realized,

96
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:41,680
 oh, I need to get a job.

97
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:42,760
 I need to get a job.

98
00:03:42,760 --> 00:03:45,920
 But I think I saw them as a sign of success.

99
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:49,800
 And, you know, that was sort of this accelerated progression

100
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:51,200
 that I was looking for.

101
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:53,120
 And then I hit the jobs.

102
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:56,720
 And I realized then that a lot of the learning

103
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:59,600
 that I was looking for didn't seem to happen every day

104
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:00,920
 in a normal job.

105
00:04:00,920 --> 00:04:04,320
 And that these ideas of kind of wait your turn,

106
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:07,400
 do your time, stay in your lane.

107
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:09,520
 Like, these started to be some of the things

108
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:10,400
 that I was seeing.

109
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:12,520
 And I forced it quite a lot.

110
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:14,520
 Like, I moved across a couple of different companies.

111
00:04:14,520 --> 00:04:16,600
 I really tried to force the point.

112
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:18,240
 I was like, I'm not sure that this is

113
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:19,960
 what a good career looks like.

114
00:04:19,960 --> 00:04:22,920
 And I had a degree of success in doing that.

115
00:04:22,920 --> 00:04:24,960
 But I think that was because I was so ambitious

116
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:26,560
 and driven and so confident.

117
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:28,960
 And I started to realize that other people

118
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:33,240
 were feeling quite stuck in traditional structures

119
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:36,480
 and they didn't have the same level of confidence

120
00:04:36,480 --> 00:04:39,640
 or support in navigating it as I did.

121
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:43,080
 And I guess it was that sort of insight

122
00:04:43,080 --> 00:04:46,600
 that was shared with my friend now, business partner,

123
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:49,320
 that led us to say, well, how can we help people?

124
00:04:49,320 --> 00:04:50,160
 What can we do?

125
00:04:50,160 --> 00:04:51,640
 And so that was about 2013,

126
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:54,680
 where we were both sharing very similar reflections

127
00:04:54,680 --> 00:04:57,760
 on our careers and our experience.

128
00:04:57,760 --> 00:05:01,040
 And also this insight that other people were finding

129
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:04,360
 the same situations but didn't have the ability

130
00:05:04,360 --> 00:05:05,840
 to change it themselves.

131
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:07,840
 They needed some help that wasn't available.

132
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:10,400
 - Well, so you saw that kind of consistent gap

133
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:12,240
 that other people were having as well.

134
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:13,560
 - Yeah, they would say I feel stuck

135
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:15,520
 and I don't know what to do about it.

136
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:16,640
 I want to do something different,

137
00:05:16,640 --> 00:05:18,520
 but I don't know how to make it happen.

138
00:05:18,520 --> 00:05:20,920
 And what also seemed to be true

139
00:05:20,920 --> 00:05:23,720
 is the more senior you got in a company,

140
00:05:23,720 --> 00:05:25,480
 the more career support you could access.

141
00:05:25,480 --> 00:05:26,560
 - Oh God, yes.

142
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:27,680
 That is so true.

143
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:30,640
 And it's almost like you want to give that

144
00:05:30,640 --> 00:05:31,880
 to people a bit sooner, right?

145
00:05:31,880 --> 00:05:32,720
 - Absolutely.

146
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:35,480
 And I really don't understand why we don't do that,

147
00:05:35,480 --> 00:05:37,520
 why the majority of the workforce

148
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:40,000
 are given the minority of the support.

149
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,240
 It doesn't stack up to me 'cause don't you want?

150
00:05:42,240 --> 00:05:43,440
 - It's almost like they don't want them

151
00:05:43,440 --> 00:05:45,440
 to have an a-ha moment too early

152
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:47,760
 'cause they wanna work 'em for a few years.

153
00:05:47,760 --> 00:05:49,240
 - Well, but then I kind of think,

154
00:05:49,240 --> 00:05:50,160
 well, if they don't have it,

155
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:51,760
 then they'll probably leave to get it elsewhere

156
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:54,120
 and water waste for everybody.

157
00:05:54,120 --> 00:05:55,760
 So we kind of had this principle

158
00:05:55,760 --> 00:05:57,760
 of well, let's democratize career development.

159
00:05:57,760 --> 00:05:59,680
 Let's not limit learning by level.

160
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:01,720
 Let's make this accessible to everybody.

161
00:06:01,720 --> 00:06:05,760
 And that was a real guiding thought in our early days.

162
00:06:05,760 --> 00:06:08,080
 And it's definitely become part of our company now,

163
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:10,280
 but I mean, we didn't have a company in 2013.

164
00:06:10,280 --> 00:06:11,560
 We just had this insight.

165
00:06:11,560 --> 00:06:12,600
 - You were doing still your job

166
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:14,160
 and you were doing a bit of it at the weekends

167
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:15,000
 and everything, right? - Yeah.

168
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,040
 They were like running like these little evening sessions

169
00:06:17,040 --> 00:06:18,360
 that six people turned up to.

170
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:19,800
 - But it's such a good way to do it.

171
00:06:19,800 --> 00:06:21,600
 You know, like, and I know we'll talk about that.

172
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:25,800
 Like, you know, how do you kind of look for opportunities?

173
00:06:25,800 --> 00:06:28,120
 Maybe when you're in a career you don't love,

174
00:06:28,120 --> 00:06:31,120
 how do you actually find, I suppose, opportunities

175
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:33,480
 to do things that you think you're gonna love more?

176
00:06:33,480 --> 00:06:34,320
 - Yeah.

177
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:35,160
 - Now, for any of our listeners

178
00:06:35,160 --> 00:06:37,000
 that never heard of the Squeakly Career,

179
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,200
 because we know some of our listeners in Australia

180
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:41,160
 and the US, et cetera, haven't.

181
00:06:41,160 --> 00:06:44,280
 How would you explain, what is a Squeakly Career?

182
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:47,120
 - Yeah, yeah, no, it's a really good, let's start there.

183
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:48,240
 Let's start there.

184
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:50,920
 So we are trying to contrast

185
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:52,920
 with the idea of the ladder-like career.

186
00:06:52,920 --> 00:06:54,480
 And so if we start there,

187
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:57,040
 the ladder-like career says that you pick a path,

188
00:06:57,040 --> 00:06:59,080
 you progress by going up the ladder,

189
00:06:59,080 --> 00:07:01,800
 you start talking about, you know, the next step.

190
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:04,200
 The assumption is that success is seniority

191
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:05,840
 and that's what everybody wants from work

192
00:07:05,840 --> 00:07:07,680
 and, you know, that promotions are possible

193
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:09,240
 'cause that's all sort of the ingredients

194
00:07:09,240 --> 00:07:11,000
 of ladder-like careers.

195
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,160
 Our perspective is that that doesn't reflect reality.

196
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:16,920
 This idea of very linear, predictable careers

197
00:07:16,920 --> 00:07:19,280
 where there are enough promotions for everybody,

198
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:22,320
 that that doesn't actually reflect people's experience.

199
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:25,840
 And it also doesn't reflect people's individuality.

200
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:28,440
 People want to do different things.

201
00:07:28,440 --> 00:07:31,920
 They don't necessarily want a career based on conformity.

202
00:07:31,920 --> 00:07:35,040
 They want the choice to develop in the direction

203
00:07:35,040 --> 00:07:35,880
 that's right for them.

204
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:39,120
 So Squeakly Career, we believe, is a better way

205
00:07:39,120 --> 00:07:41,040
 of people thinking about their career.

206
00:07:41,040 --> 00:07:43,360
 It gives them the freedom and flexibility

207
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:45,160
 to develop in different directions.

208
00:07:45,160 --> 00:07:48,960
 But it also, it presents to people that actually

209
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:51,320
 your career is probably not going to be predictable.

210
00:07:51,320 --> 00:07:52,880
 You and I are going to need to develop skills

211
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:54,040
 we have not got yet.

212
00:07:54,040 --> 00:07:55,720
 We are potentially going to do jobs

213
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:57,320
 that don't even exist yet.

214
00:07:57,320 --> 00:08:00,840
 And if we are open to that, we'll create more opportunities.

215
00:08:00,840 --> 00:08:04,240
 But if we are close to that, if we're not proactive,

216
00:08:04,240 --> 00:08:06,240
 if we're not exploring, if we're not curious,

217
00:08:06,240 --> 00:08:08,280
 then actually our careers become quite vulnerable

218
00:08:08,280 --> 00:08:10,960
 and we don't want people to be vulnerable

219
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:12,360
 in a context of change.

220
00:08:12,360 --> 00:08:17,080
 We want people to use that change to fuel their career

221
00:08:17,080 --> 00:08:20,040
 rather than mean that it sort of leaves them feeling lost.

222
00:08:20,040 --> 00:08:20,960
 That's not what we want.

223
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:23,280
 - I love what you, I heard you say this

224
00:08:23,280 --> 00:08:24,880
 on another podcast recently.

225
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:28,200
 You said think, I think you said think talent not titles.

226
00:08:28,200 --> 00:08:30,440
 And I thought it was really interesting

227
00:08:30,440 --> 00:08:32,480
 'cause what you're trying to get people to think about

228
00:08:32,480 --> 00:08:35,280
 is what are their unique talents

229
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:38,000
 and actually how can you accelerate those

230
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:39,920
 as opposed to thinking in,

231
00:08:39,920 --> 00:08:41,800
 I've got to reach this next title.

232
00:08:41,800 --> 00:08:45,040
 - Yeah, well, there's a lot of ladder like language

233
00:08:45,040 --> 00:08:46,480
 that goes along with that concept.

234
00:08:46,480 --> 00:08:48,440
 So you end up, if you look at careers like ladders,

235
00:08:48,440 --> 00:08:50,280
 you end up with words like,

236
00:08:50,280 --> 00:08:52,480
 or kind of a fixation on titles.

237
00:08:52,480 --> 00:08:54,840
 And everything has to sound similar

238
00:08:54,840 --> 00:08:57,400
 and a bit more senior to what I currently do.

239
00:08:57,400 --> 00:09:00,680
 So you get the kind of junior executive direct,

240
00:09:00,680 --> 00:09:02,760
 it's all in the same path, all a bit more senior.

241
00:09:02,760 --> 00:09:04,760
 And you end up talking about plans,

242
00:09:04,760 --> 00:09:06,440
 the perfect career plan.

243
00:09:06,440 --> 00:09:08,880
 You talk about promotions, you talk about levels,

244
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:10,400
 you talk about grades.

245
00:09:10,400 --> 00:09:13,400
 But when you flip to squiggly thinking,

246
00:09:13,400 --> 00:09:15,280
 you don't talk about levels, you talk about learning,

247
00:09:15,280 --> 00:09:17,120
 you don't talk about grades, you talk about growth,

248
00:09:17,120 --> 00:09:18,960
 you don't talk about titles, you talk about talents,

249
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:21,200
 you don't talk about plans, you talk about possibilities.

250
00:09:21,200 --> 00:09:25,640
 It's just, it's a much more curious and open way

251
00:09:25,640 --> 00:09:26,880
 of looking at your career.

252
00:09:26,880 --> 00:09:30,440
 And the language is actually a really fundamental part

253
00:09:30,440 --> 00:09:32,400
 'cause if you were my manager,

254
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:34,240
 I'd be talking to you about my career.

255
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:36,720
 And if I talked to you about the next job title I want

256
00:09:36,720 --> 00:09:40,480
 in Kahal's company or the next level I want to get to,

257
00:09:40,480 --> 00:09:42,280
 it's quite a closed conversation.

258
00:09:42,280 --> 00:09:45,160
 But if I talk to you about the talents I want to be known for

259
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:47,600
 and the learning I want to gain so I can grow,

260
00:09:47,600 --> 00:09:49,800
 very different conversation.

261
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:52,280
 - Yeah, it's interesting 'cause having myself worked

262
00:09:52,280 --> 00:09:54,160
 in large companies for too long,

263
00:09:54,160 --> 00:09:58,640
