 I'll just apply for that.

1033
00:39:18,000 --> 00:39:21,320
 So the first is you've got to give people greater awareness

1034
00:39:21,320 --> 00:39:23,600
 of the opportunities in an organization

1035
00:39:23,600 --> 00:39:25,640
 where their talents could take them to a job,

1036
00:39:25,640 --> 00:39:27,280
 not just their titles.

1037
00:39:27,280 --> 00:39:29,200
 And the second is access.

1038
00:39:29,200 --> 00:39:32,760
 So we make it very hard for people to get to new jobs.

1039
00:39:32,760 --> 00:39:35,080
 So you've got to be here for two and a half years.

1040
00:39:35,080 --> 00:39:36,920
 You've got to go through 10 interviews.

1041
00:39:36,920 --> 00:39:38,680
 You've got to have this rating.

1042
00:39:38,680 --> 00:39:40,360
 And then you go, well, I'll just apply for that job.

1043
00:39:40,360 --> 00:39:41,640
 I don't have to do any of those things.

1044
00:39:41,640 --> 00:39:44,120
 I know that I could do it and I'll go there.

1045
00:39:44,120 --> 00:39:45,760
 So we need to make it easier.

1046
00:39:45,760 --> 00:39:47,960
 We kind of say like try before you apply.

1047
00:39:47,960 --> 00:39:52,200
 How can you spend a week working in that team

1048
00:39:52,200 --> 00:39:53,680
 or get some exposure on a project,

1049
00:39:53,680 --> 00:39:55,280
 like help people to try before they apply

1050
00:39:55,280 --> 00:39:56,800
 and make access easier?

1051
00:39:56,800 --> 00:39:59,040
 And then the third reason is advocacy.

1052
00:39:59,040 --> 00:40:01,360
 So even if I can see what I can be

1053
00:40:01,360 --> 00:40:04,840
 and I've sort of tried it and I know I want to apply,

1054
00:40:04,840 --> 00:40:07,960
 I might need somebody to speak up for me and say,

1055
00:40:07,960 --> 00:40:10,280
 well, I know Helen's not worked in procurement,

1056
00:40:10,280 --> 00:40:12,440
 but I have seen that she's really commercial.

1057
00:40:12,440 --> 00:40:14,440
 I've seen that with her work in marketing

1058
00:40:14,440 --> 00:40:15,880
 and I think we should give her a chance

1059
00:40:15,880 --> 00:40:17,600
 or give her the opportunity.

1060
00:40:17,600 --> 00:40:20,560
 If you can crack awareness, access and advocacy,

1061
00:40:20,560 --> 00:40:21,680
 people will stay.

1062
00:40:21,680 --> 00:40:24,160
 But most people aren't trying to sort of cracking

1063
00:40:24,160 --> 00:40:25,480
 those things and so people just,

1064
00:40:25,480 --> 00:40:28,240
 it's just easy for me to look somewhere else and leave.

1065
00:40:28,240 --> 00:40:29,240
 - And that's really sad

1066
00:40:29,240 --> 00:40:31,280
 'cause I think then organizations do lose

1067
00:40:31,280 --> 00:40:32,520
 some amazing people. - Absolutely.

1068
00:40:32,520 --> 00:40:34,280
 - And I saw some great stats on your website,

1069
00:40:34,280 --> 00:40:36,480
 like people starting work today

1070
00:40:36,480 --> 00:40:39,000
 will have twice as many jobs over their careers

1071
00:40:39,000 --> 00:40:41,480
 compared to 15 years ago.

1072
00:40:41,480 --> 00:40:45,560
 And I also saw nearly 50% of people say

1073
00:40:45,560 --> 00:40:47,560
 they would leave an organization

1074
00:40:47,560 --> 00:40:50,760
 where they can't progress in some way.

1075
00:40:50,760 --> 00:40:53,400
 - Yeah, I think people want to progress.

1076
00:40:53,400 --> 00:40:55,680
 I think we did this really fun exercise

1077
00:40:55,680 --> 00:40:57,440
 in some of our sessions

1078
00:40:57,440 --> 00:40:59,480
 where we call that progression bingo.

1079
00:40:59,480 --> 00:41:02,240
 And so obviously when you're looking at careers

1080
00:41:02,240 --> 00:41:04,680
 like a ladder, the only way you progress is getting promoted.

1081
00:41:04,680 --> 00:41:07,160
 Our kind of premises and squiggly careers,

1082
00:41:07,160 --> 00:41:08,360
 there's lots of ways you can progress.

1083
00:41:08,360 --> 00:41:10,640
 So we get everyone to basically fill out the bingo card

1084
00:41:10,640 --> 00:41:12,720
 while all the ways in which you can progress

1085
00:41:12,720 --> 00:41:16,280
 and they'll talk about, oh, I could shadow someone,

1086
00:41:16,280 --> 00:41:17,520
 I could go on a succumbent,

1087
00:41:17,520 --> 00:41:19,320
 I could work on a project, loads of ways.

1088
00:41:19,320 --> 00:41:22,240
 And then, and obviously promotion is one of those ways.

1089
00:41:22,240 --> 00:41:24,640
 And then we ask people, this is a bit that I really love,

1090
00:41:24,640 --> 00:41:26,760
 what do you want to do most now?

1091
00:41:26,760 --> 00:41:29,480
 And hardly anyone ever says promotion.

1092
00:41:29,480 --> 00:41:31,840
 They go, actually, I'd quite like to do a squiggly succumbent.

1093
00:41:31,840 --> 00:41:33,920
 I'd quite like to go spend nine months in a different role

1094
00:41:33,920 --> 00:41:34,960
 and just see what it's like,

1095
00:41:34,960 --> 00:41:37,360
 or I'd like to go just do 50/50.

1096
00:41:37,360 --> 00:41:38,600
 I'd like to do a bit of a job share.

1097
00:41:38,600 --> 00:41:40,400
 I'd like to do 50% of my time there.

1098
00:41:40,400 --> 00:41:42,920
 And it's like, well, let's get a bit more creative

1099
00:41:42,920 --> 00:41:44,800
 with what career development can look like.

1100
00:41:44,800 --> 00:41:47,320
 And people want to progress.

1101
00:41:47,320 --> 00:41:51,200
 They've only been given promotion as the way of doing it.

1102
00:41:51,200 --> 00:41:52,080
 - I completely agree.

1103
00:41:52,080 --> 00:41:55,160
 And I used to see sometimes in big organizations,

1104
00:41:55,160 --> 00:41:59,080
 managers think they own the people in the team.

1105
00:41:59,080 --> 00:42:00,480
 And I used to say to my peers,

1106
00:42:00,480 --> 00:42:01,960
 like, we don't own these people.

1107
00:42:01,960 --> 00:42:04,680
 You know, like, Helen is really good at that.

1108
00:42:04,680 --> 00:42:07,240
 I am going to allow her to have a succumbent over there

1109
00:42:07,240 --> 00:42:10,440
 because I think that's great for the organization.

1110
00:42:10,440 --> 00:42:12,880
 But I think sometimes people get a bit territorial,

1111
00:42:12,880 --> 00:42:14,160
 even on their people.

1112
00:42:14,160 --> 00:42:16,720
 They don't want, they go, well, I invested them.

1113
00:42:16,720 --> 00:42:18,200
 I brought them in.

1114
00:42:18,200 --> 00:42:20,560
 I don't want them going off doing something that,

1115
00:42:20,560 --> 00:42:22,280
 you know, I didn't have to go and hire another person.

1116
00:42:22,280 --> 00:42:24,960
 - Yeah, I think metrics don't help this.

1117
00:42:24,960 --> 00:42:27,920
 So we have created lots of structures

1118
00:42:27,920 --> 00:42:31,280
 where the metrics that managers are measured against

1119
00:42:31,280 --> 00:42:32,880
 detract from them,

1120
00:42:32,880 --> 00:42:34,920
 letting people go and develop in different directions.

1121
00:42:34,920 --> 00:42:37,400
 'Cause they go, well, you're such a strong performer.

1122
00:42:37,400 --> 00:42:38,600
 I need you to stay in my team

1123
00:42:38,600 --> 00:42:40,840
 because your performance determines my performance.

1124
00:42:40,840 --> 00:42:43,200
 And therefore, like, you're not going anywhere.

1125
00:42:43,200 --> 00:42:44,840
 And you get this kind of talent hoarding thing

1126
00:42:44,840 --> 00:42:45,960
 that happens. - Totally.

1127
00:42:45,960 --> 00:42:48,480
 - Whereas if we had some mobility metrics,

1128
00:42:48,480 --> 00:42:50,400
 so if there were manager metrics

1129
00:42:50,400 --> 00:42:52,840
 that were more about how many people have moved

1130
00:42:52,840 --> 00:42:55,080
 into other areas in the business from your team,

1131
00:42:55,080 --> 00:42:56,280
 how many people who have done

1132
00:42:56,280 --> 00:42:57,760
 what we call a squiggly safari,

1133
00:42:57,760 --> 00:42:59,720
 where they'll have spent time in different teams,

1134
00:42:59,720 --> 00:43:02,000
 if we had some more mobility metrics,

1135
00:43:02,000 --> 00:43:04,960
 I think there might be a greater appetite for managers

1136
00:43:04,960 --> 00:43:08,240
 to, you know, positively support some of these things.

1137
00:43:08,240 --> 00:43:09,320
 - I agree, I agree.

1138
00:43:09,320 --> 00:43:12,400
 And because sometimes some of the other metrics you have is,

1139
00:43:12,400 --> 00:43:15,040
 you know, managers get very worried

1140
00:43:15,040 --> 00:43:17,680
 if people are not staying in their team for a long time,

1141
00:43:17,680 --> 00:43:19,640
 right, like, or-- - What does it say about me?

1142
00:43:19,640 --> 00:43:21,200
 - Got high turnover in the team, whatever.

1143
00:43:21,200 --> 00:43:22,280
 So I agree.

1144
00:43:22,280 --> 00:43:24,160
 I think there's a little bit of work to do around that

1145
00:43:24,160 --> 00:43:25,520
 in big organizations.

1146
00:43:25,520 --> 00:43:28,360
 Now, we're on to our final few questions.

1147
00:43:28,360 --> 00:43:30,600
 Is there anything happening in the world of careers right now

1148
00:43:30,600 --> 00:43:32,120
 that's really exciting you?

1149
00:43:32,120 --> 00:43:35,160
 - Oh, that's a lovely, that's a lovely question.

1150
00:43:35,160 --> 00:43:36,640
 What's exciting me in the world?

1151
00:43:36,640 --> 00:43:41,520
 I think I'm just, I think one of the most exciting things,

1152
00:43:41,520 --> 00:43:43,360
 and everyone's talking about AI.

1153
00:43:43,360 --> 00:43:44,680
 So it's not AI specifically,

1154
00:43:44,680 --> 00:43:47,760
 but it's just this recognition of

1155
00:43:47,760 --> 00:43:50,760
 that skills need to be updated.

1156
00:43:50,760 --> 00:43:55,760
 Like, I feel like skills being in a boardroom conversation

1157
00:43:55,760 --> 00:43:59,920
 is, that is exciting to me, like, that people are saying,

1158
00:43:59,920 --> 00:44:01,920
 you know, what's the skill status in our organization?

1159
00:44:01,920 --> 00:44:03,760
 What new skills do we want to develop?

1160
00:44:03,760 --> 00:44:05,240
 Where have we got skill gaps?

1161
00:44:05,240 --> 00:44:07,520
 Like, I just think this is becoming

1162
00:44:07,520 --> 00:44:10,760
 a much more significant conversation.

1163
00:44:10,760 --> 00:44:12,720
 And as somebody who is very, very passionate

1164
00:44:12,720 --> 00:44:15,120
 about learning at work, like, I care about careers

1165
00:44:15,120 --> 00:44:16,920
 and I care about learning at work,

1166
00:44:16,920 --> 00:44:21,920
 I feel like they are becoming a bit more significant

1167
00:44:21,920 --> 00:44:23,440
 in very senior discussions.

1168
00:44:23,440 --> 00:44:25,600
 And I am all, I am all for that.

1169
00:44:25,600 --> 00:44:26,640
 - I'm so with you.

1170
00:44:26,640 --> 00:44:28,200
 I think the same.

1171
00:44:28,200 --> 00:44:31,200
 Skills, it's like at the table for once.

1172
00:44:31,200 --> 00:44:32,040
 - Yeah, yeah.

1173
00:44:32,040 --> 00:44:34,600
 - Similar for me, I'm very passionate about leadership

1174
00:44:34,600 --> 00:44:38,280
 and leadership is now being discussed a lot, right?

1175
00:44:38,280 --> 00:44:40,560
 You know, how can we have better leaders?

1176
00:44:40,560 --> 00:44:43,240
 How do we set them up for success, et cetera?

1177
00:44:43,240 --> 00:44:44,680
 So we're getting a lot of calls

1178
00:44:44,680 --> 00:44:47,440
 about they helped me build a program, helped me do this.

1179
00:44:47,440 --> 00:44:48,920
 I'm like, oh my God, what's happening?

1180
00:44:48,920 --> 00:44:49,760
 - Yeah, yeah, it's brilliant.

1181
00:44:49,760 --> 00:44:50,600
 - So it's exciting, right?

1182
00:44:50,600 --> 00:44:51,440
 - Yeah, it's really exciting.

1183
00:44:51,440 --> 00:44:54,640
 - Now we ask all of our guests these final three questions.

1184
00:44:54,640 --> 00:44:56,520
 What's the smallest possible change you think

1185
00:44:56,520 --> 00:44:58,480
 our listeners could do to have an impact

1186
00:44:58,480 --> 00:45:00,240
 and a better day at work tomorrow?

1187
00:45:00,240 --> 00:45:04,000
 - I think the smallest things they could do

1188
00:45:04,000 --> 00:45:08,080
 is to start reflecting on their,

1189
00:45:08,080 --> 00:45:09,640
 what we call your very small successes

1190
00:45:09,640 --> 00:45:10,800
 or what you do well at work.

1191
00:45:10,800 --> 00:45:13,120
 And the reason to do this is, you know,

1192
00:45:13,120 --> 00:45:14,560
 I said that confidence gremlins are getting

1193
00:45:14,560 --> 00:45:15,840
 in most people's way.

1194
00:45:15,840 --> 00:45:18,040
 So that's where you're kind of be telling yourself

1195
00:45:18,040 --> 00:45:20,200
 and kind of negative story about what you're doing.

1196
00:45:20,200 --> 00:45:22,000
 I think if we start to kind of just say

1197
00:45:22,000 --> 00:45:23,680
 at the end of the day, what did I do well today?

1198
00:45:23,680 --> 00:45:24,840
 What did I do well today?

1199
00:45:24,840 --> 00:45:28,040
 Not only are you likely to start spotting some strengths

1200
00:45:28,040 --> 00:45:31,080
 of like, hmm, I did that well because I used that skill.

1201
00:45:31,080 --> 00:45:33,840
 I think you also start in a small but cumulative way

1202
00:45:33,840 --> 00:45:34,920
 to build yourself belief.

1203
00:45:34,920 --> 00:45:36,040
 So what did I do well today?

1204
00:45:36,040 --> 00:45:37,520
 I think is a useful reflection.

1205
00:45:37,520 --> 00:45:39,560
 - Love that, okay, I'm gonna try that tonight.

1206
00:45:39,560 --> 00:45:42,160
 I did not do well with bringing my iPad today

1207
00:45:42,160 --> 00:45:43,040
 to this interview.

1208
00:45:43,040 --> 00:45:44,800
 So I had to borrow Helen's one.

1209
00:45:44,800 --> 00:45:45,640
 - People helping people.

1210
00:45:45,640 --> 00:45:47,080
 - People helping people.

1211
00:45:47,080 --> 00:45:49,320
 Can you share with our listeners something you learned

1212
00:45:49,320 --> 00:45:51,480
 or experienced at work that unexpectedly

1213
00:45:51,480 --> 00:45:53,120
 made your whole life better?

1214
00:45:53,120 --> 00:45:56,360
 - Oh, I mean, my business partner.

1215
00:45:56,360 --> 00:45:57,360
 I learned my business partner,

1216
00:45:57,360 --> 00:45:58,720
 but what I specifically learned

1217
00:45:58,720 --> 00:46:01,280
 and it has made my whole work and my life better

1218
00:46:01,280 --> 00:46:04,400
 is she is my opposite in every way.

1219
00:46:04,400 --> 00:46:06,520
 She is an introvert, I'm an extrovert.

1220
00:46:06,520 --> 00:46:08,560
 She is very thoughtful and reflective.

1221
00:46:08,560 --> 00:46:10,160
 I'm all about action.

1222
00:46:10,160 --> 00:46:15,160
 And the compliments of people who are your opposite

1223
00:46:15,160 --> 00:46:18,200
 I think can be amazing.

1224
00:46:18,200 --> 00:46:21,840
 Like we run a better business because of our differences.

1225
00:46:21,840 --> 00:46:24,720
 And I think it is very easy to work with people

1226
00:46:24,720 --> 00:46:27,040
 who are similar to you that you feel comfortable with.

1227
00:46:27,040 --> 00:46:29,000
 But sometimes those points of difference

1228
00:46:29,000 --> 00:46:30,680
 are what makes you better.

1229
00:46:30,680 --> 00:46:32,680
 And I'm definitely better because of her.

1230
00:46:32,680 --> 00:46:35,560
 - I love that, I love that, I hope she's listened.

1231
00:46:35,560 --> 00:46:36,400
 - To this bit, not the first bit.

1232
00:46:36,400 --> 00:46:39,600
 - Exactly, yeah, not the first bit about the values.

1233
00:46:39,600 --> 00:46:41,840
 We finish all interviews with this question.

1234
00:46:41,840 --> 00:46:44,800
 Can you recall, Helen, the best advice you received

1235
00:46:44,800 --> 00:46:47,440
 that you think makes you better at work?

1236
00:46:47,440 --> 00:46:48,560
 - I can recall the advice,

1237
00:46:48,560 --> 00:46:50,880
 but I cannot recall where it came from,

1238
00:46:50,880 --> 00:46:54,480
 but it goes through my head almost every day.

1239
00:46:54,480 --> 00:46:57,440
 And the advice is to run your own race.

1240
00:46:57,440 --> 00:47:02,440
 And I find that stops me ever needing to compete or compare.

1241
00:47:02,440 --> 00:47:06,040
 It just, it helps me go, well, you know, use your values.

1242
00:47:06,040 --> 00:47:06,920
 If you're running your own race,

1243
00:47:06,920 --> 00:47:09,120
 you're using your values to run that race.

1244
00:47:09,120 --> 00:47:12,480
 And you can define your own measures of success.

1245
00:47:12,480 --> 00:47:16,360
 And it just keeps me on my own path at my own pace,

1246
00:47:16,360 --> 00:47:18,800
 not trying to, you know, worry about other people.

1247
00:47:18,800 --> 00:47:20,280
 - I love that, I love that.

1248
00:47:20,280 --> 00:47:23,440
 Thank you so much. - Oh, thank you.

